<figure class="miss-figure">
 <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 4</span>Refresh before the old answer becomes unusable</div>
 <div class="scene">
  <div class="timeline"><div><strong>t = 0 to 24 s</strong><span>Serve the existing card while it remains permitted.</span></div><div class="refresh"><strong>t = 24 to 30 s</strong><span>A request may start one refresh. Other callers can still receive the old card.</span></div></div>
  <div class="end"><strong>At t = 30 s: stop serving this old card</strong><p>This boundary is fixed by the freshness rule, even if the refresh has failed.</p></div>
  <ol class="trace">
   <li><time>24 s</time><div><strong>A request for P7 starts a refresh</strong><span>The caller receives the still-permitted card without waiting for that refresh.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>25 s</time><div><strong>Another P7 request arrives</strong><span>It also receives the old card. It sees a refresh in flight and starts none.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>26 s</time><div><strong>Suppose the refresh fails</strong><span>Record the failure. The old answer’s allowed serving time does not move.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>30 s</time><div><strong>A new caller cannot use the old answer</strong><span>It must enter the bounded miss path or receive a permitted reduced result or failure.</span></div></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="explanation">If the refresh succeeds, later callers can use its replacement after validation. If nobody requests this key after 24 s, this request-triggered design starts no refresh.</p>
 </div>
 <figcaption>The clock and refresh point are invented. The freshness policy has already established t = 30 s as this answer’s serving boundary. It is not inferred from the baseline thirty-second insertion TTL, and invalidation may end permission sooner.</figcaption>
</figure>
